\subsection{CSV profiles}{

The CSV backend resolves every canonical column through the schema, so the
profile has to say what your columns are called. Name them under
\verb{schema: columns:}, keyed by tidycreel's canonical name with no \verb{_col}
suffix:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{schema:
  survey_type: instantaneous
  columns:
    interview_uid: InterviewID
    date: SurveyDate
    effort: HoursFished
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A column the source already names the way tidycreel does needs no entry: the
fetch takes it by its canonical name and says which columns it took that way.
Declare it anyway to state the mapping explicitly. A column that is neither
mapped nor canonically named is dropped on the way in, which
\code{fetch_interviews()} reports as a missing required column. Note that an entry
naming a column the source does not have stays a drop — it is not rescued by
a canonically-named column, because a mapping that does not match is a
configuration error worth seeing. A commented template is installed at

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{system.file("extdata", "csv-profile-example.yml", package = "tidycreel.connect")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{backend: api} ignores column mappings and reads raw JSON keys from
\code{field_map}, so a \verb{columns:} block there is an error rather than a no-op.

\code{backend: sqlserver} resolves a \verb{columns:} block exactly as \code{csv} does: both
read a whole table and rename it through the schema. It additionally needs
the table names, which the same \verb{schema:} block carries as
\code{interviews_table}, \code{counts_table}, \code{catch_table} and
\code{harvest_lengths_table} / \code{release_lengths_table} (both falling back to
\code{lengths_table}).

A SQL Server connection is encrypted and verifies the server's certificate
unless the profile says otherwise (#31), because the login and the rows it
returns -- which can include personal data -- otherwise cross the network in
cleartext. Three optional keys control it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{driver: ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server   # the default
encrypt: true                           # the default
trust_server_certificate: false         # the default
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{encrypt: false} warns (\code{creel_warning_sqlserver_unencrypted}) on every
connect, and so does \code{trust_server_certificate: true}
(\code{creel_warning_sqlserver_certificate_unverified}), which encrypts but
accepts any certificate. Driver 17 does not encrypt unless asked, so naming
it keeps \code{encrypt: true} in force. When a connect fails because the driver
is not installed, the error says so; see \code{\link[=creel_check_driver]{creel_check_driver()}}.
}

\subsection{Corrections}{

An optional top-level \verb{corrections:} key names a CSV of documented fixes to
the fetched data, read relative to the profile's directory:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{corrections: corrections.csv
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{creel_uid,table,date,field,from,to,reason
,counts,2025-06-14,period,1,2,Section 3 counted PM; sheet says AM
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each row changes \code{field} from \code{from} to \code{to} on the \code{table} records of that
\code{date} (\code{interviews} or \code{counts}; catch and lengths carry no date). Names
and values are tidycreel's, after \code{field_map}/\code{columns} and \code{value_maps}.
Every row needs a \code{reason}. Leave \code{creel_uid} blank on a CSV or SQL Server
profile, or on an API profile with one creel; name the creel on an API
profile with several. Rows for creels the profile does not fetch are
ignored, so one file can serve them all.

\code{NA} in \code{from} matches a missing value and in \code{to} writes one. Quote a
value that contains a comma.

\code{fetch_interviews()} and \code{fetch_counts()} apply them in file order, before
validation, and list each change once per connection
(\code{creel_message_corrections_applied}) by line, date, field and count. The
values themselves are not printed, since one can be personal (a ZIP code);
keep the \code{reason} free of them too. The result carries the rows applied,
with the number of records each changed, as \code{attr(x, "corrections")}, for a
report to list. A correction that matches no record is an error
(\code{creel_error_correction_unmatched}): the data changed underneath it, and it
has to be checked again rather than silently do nothing.
}

\subsection{Cleanup}{

An optional top-level \verb{cleanup:} block removes rows that are artifacts of
how the source serves data rather than records. Every step is off unless
named \code{true}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{cleanup:
  interviews:
    drop_placeholders: true
    collapse_repeats: true
  counts:
    drop_placeholders: true
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{drop_placeholders} drops rows that carry a value in no column but
\code{creel_uid}, \code{date} and the strata: rows a source writes for a sampled date
on which nothing was recorded. A blank string counts as no value. Rows are
judged on the fetched columns, so a source column that holds data the
analysis needs must be mapped to count. A blank count row is dropped, never read as
a zero. An interview row with no \code{interview_uid} that does carry data is an
error (\code{creel_error_cleanup_rows_without_uid}), not a placeholder.

\code{collapse_repeats} keeps one of each set of interview rows identical in
every fetched column: a long-format source repeats each interview once per
questionnaire answer, which \code{fetch_interviews()} otherwise warns about
(\code{creel_warning_fetch_created_duplicates}) and returns. If one interview
still has two rows that differ, the fetch aborts
(\code{creel_error_cleanup_conflicting_repeats}) and names the columns, since
choosing one would be a guess. Rows with no \code{interview_uid} are refused
before collapsing (\code{creel_error_cleanup_rows_without_uid}), since nothing
says identical ones are one interview; set \code{drop_placeholders} too when they
are placeholders. Counts have no \code{collapse_repeats}: two
identical count rows can be two real counts.

Cleanup runs after corrections. The fetch reports what it removed once per
connection (\code{creel_message_rows_cleaned}), and the result carries the
counts as \code{attr(x, "cleanup")}: \code{rows_in}, \code{placeholders_dropped},
\code{repeats_collapsed} and \code{rows_kept}.
}

